{#- ===========================================================
    Menu — large print

    WHY THIS EXISTS. The tri-fold is a good piece of paper and it is also
    about as small as type can honestly go: three panels on a letter sheet
    puts item descriptions near 7pt, and this cafe's customers skew older.
    Rather than wreck the tri-fold trying to make it do both jobs — its
    panels are a fixed height and do not scroll, so bigger type there means
    clipped text, not a longer panel — this is a second piece with the same
    data and a different geometry.

    TWO COLUMNS, NOT THREE, on a portrait sheet, and THREE SHEETS rather
    than two. That is where the room for bigger type comes from; there is no
    other trick here. Descriptions run about 10pt against the tri-fold's
    7.5pt, at full contrast rather than the 0.75 opacity a dense panel needs.

    The page count is the honest cost of the type size, and it is the right
    trade for this piece: a large-print menu squeezed back onto two sheets is
    just the tri-fold again. Plain paper, no folding, so an extra sheet costs
    a sheet. The first proof of this piece WAS two pages and clipped three
    categories off the bottom - a fixed artboard clips, it does not reflow.

    SAME DATA, so this cannot drift from the tri-fold, /menu or the board.
    Everything below reads src/_data/menu.json. If you find yourself typing
    an item name into this file, stop — it belongs in the data.

    COLUMN ASSIGNMENT is by hand at the foot of this file, like the board's.
    The sheet does not scroll: a column that overruns 11in is clipped, not
    continued. If you add items, move a whole category and re-proof the
    export.
    =========================================================== #}

{%- set breakfast = (menu.categories | where("id", "breakfast"))[0] %}
{%- set breakfastSides = (menu.categories | where("id", "breakfast_sides"))[0] %}
{%- set wraps = (menu.categories | where("id", "wraps"))[0] %}
{%- set sandwiches = (menu.categories | where("id", "sandwiches"))[0] %}
{%- set hotSandwiches = (menu.categories | where("id", "hot_sandwiches"))[0] %}
{%- set burgers = (menu.categories | where("id", "burgers"))[0] %}
{%- set salads = (menu.categories | where("id", "salads"))[0] %}
{%- set soup = (menu.categories | where("id", "soup"))[0] %}
{%- set kids = (menu.categories | where("id", "kids"))[0] %}
{%- set sides = (menu.categories | where("id", "sides"))[0] %}
{%- set drinks = (menu.categories | where("id", "drinks"))[0] %}

{#- The marks, the key and the category renderer are the tri-fold's, class
    for class, on purpose: the stylesheet is what differs between the two
    pieces, not the markup. Copying the markup means copying the decisions
    about mark order and about `diet` versus `dietOnRequest`, and two copies
    of a decision is one copy too many. -#}
{%- macro dietMarks(item) %}
{%- set served = item.diet or [] %}
{%- set onRequest = item.dietOnRequest or [] %}
{%- if served.length or onRequest.length -%}
<span class="menu-item-diet">
    {%- for d in menu.diets -%}
        {%- if d.code in served %}<abbr class="diet-mark" title="{{ d.name }}">{{ d.code }}</abbr>
        {%- elif d.code in onRequest %}<abbr class="diet-mark diet-mark--request" title="{{ d.name }} on request">{{ d.code }}*</abbr>
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
</span>
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro dietLegend() %}
<div class="diet-legend">
    <p class="p-label diet-legend-title ink-accent">Dietary key</p>
    <p class="p-body diet-legend-keys">
        {%- for d in menu.diets %}<span class="diet-mark">{{ d.code }}</span>&nbsp;{{ d.name }}{% if not loop.last %} &middot; {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
    </p>
    <p class="p-body diet-legend-note"><span class="diet-mark">*</span> we can make it that way. Just ask.</p>
    <p class="p-body diet-legend-caveat">{{ menu.dietNote }}</p>
    {#- The consumer advisory. Its own paragraph, and the LAST one, because it
        is the only line in this block that is not about us — the rest explain
        our marks and our kitchen, this one is a warning. It prints on every
        sheet and is never gated behind dietsConfirmed: see _note_health. -#}
    <p class="p-body diet-legend-advisory">{{ menu.healthNote }}</p>
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{%- macro category(c) %}
<div class="menu-cat">
    <h3 class="menu-cat-name p-display ink-teal">{{ c.name }}</h3>
    {%- if c.serving %}
    <p class="menu-cat-sizing menu-cat-serving p-label ink-accent">{{ c.serving }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.note %}
    <p class="menu-cat-note p-body">{{ c.note }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.sizing %}
    <p class="menu-cat-sizing p-label ink-accent">
        {%- for s in c.sizing %}{{ s.size }} {{ s.price | money }}{% if not loop.last %} &middot; {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.items %}
    <ul class="menu-items">
        {%- for item in c.items %}
        <li class="menu-item">
            <div class="menu-item-head">
                <span class="menu-item-name p-body">{{ item.name }}
                    {{- dietMarks(item) }}
                    {%- if item.priceReview %}<span class="price-review" title="Price not re-costed yet">&bull;</span>{% endif -%}
                </span>
                {%- if not item.variants %}
                <span class="menu-item-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {%- endif %}
                {%- if item.price %}
                <span class="menu-item-price p-body">{{ item.price | money }}</span>
                {%- elif item.prices %}
                <span class="menu-item-price p-body">
                    {%- for pr in item.prices %}{{ pr | money }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
                </span>
                {%- endif %}
            </div>
            {%- if item.description %}
            <p class="menu-item-desc p-body">{{ item.description }}</p>
            {%- endif %}
            {#- One indented priced row per variant — see the note in
                menu-trifold.njk for why this is not a string in the price
                column. -#}
            {%- if item.variants %}
            <ul class="menu-variants">
                {%- for v in item.variants %}
                <li class="menu-variant">
                    <span class="menu-variant-name p-body">{{ v.size }}</span>
                    <span class="menu-item-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="menu-item-price p-body">{{ v.price | money }}</span>
                </li>
                {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
            {%- endif %}
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.picks %}
    <div class="menu-picks">
        {%- for p in c.picks %}
        <div class="menu-pick">
            <p class="menu-pick-label p-label ink-accent">{{ p.label }}</p>
            <ul class="menu-pick-list">
                {%- for o in p.options %}
                <li class="menu-pick-option p-body">{{ o }}</li>
                {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

<div class="lp-stack{% if not menu.dietsConfirmed %} diets-unconfirmed{% endif %}">

    {%- if not menu.dietsConfirmed %}
    <p class="fold-legend fold-legend--review fold-legend--warn">Dietary marks are drafted from the ingredient lists and are <strong>not kitchen-confirmed</strong> &mdash; do not send this to a printer until they are. Set <code>dietsConfirmed</code> in menu.json to clear this. Screen and PNG proof only.</p>
    {%- endif %}

    <p class="fold-legend fold-legend--review">A red <span class="price-review">&bull;</span> marks a price still to be re-costed. Screen and PNG proof only &mdash; it never prints.</p>

    <p class="fold-legend">Page 1 &mdash; masthead, breakfast &amp; the cold case</p>

    {#- PAGE 1. Carries the masthead, because a sheet handed across a counter
        has to say whose menu it is even when page 2 walks off on its own —
        which is also why page 2 repeats the name in a thin rule at its head. -#}
    <div class="canvas lp-canvas">
        <header class="lp-head">
            <div class="wordmark wordmark--ink lp-wordmark">
                {% inlineSvg "/assets/thecafe.svg", "", site.name %}
            </div>
            <div class="lp-head-meta">
                <p class="p-display-italic lp-tagline ink-teal">Breakfast &amp; Lunch</p>
                <p class="p-body lp-head-line">{{ site.opening.hoursDays }} &middot; {{ site.opening.opens.time }} {{ site.opening.opens.meridiem }} &ndash; {{ site.opening.closes.time }} {{ site.opening.closes.meridiem }}</p>
                <p class="p-body lp-head-line lp-head-phone ink-teal">{{ site.phone }} &middot; {{ site.address.street }}</p>
            </div>
        </header>

        <div class="lp-cols">
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(breakfast) }}
                {{ category(breakfastSides) }}

                <div class="panel-spacer"></div>
                {{ dietLegend() }}
            </div>
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(wraps) }}
                {{ category(sandwiches) }}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="fold-legend">Page 2 &mdash; the griddle &amp; the salads</p>

    {#- PAGE 2. Burgers and the hot sandwiches share a column — same
        flat-top, one decision (see _note_hot_cold in menu.json). -#}
    <div class="canvas lp-canvas">
        <header class="lp-head lp-head--run">
            <p class="p-display lp-runhead ink-teal">{{ site.name }} &middot; Menu</p>
            <p class="p-body lp-head-line lp-head-phone ink-teal">{{ site.phone }} &middot; {{ site.social.website }}</p>
        </header>

        <div class="lp-cols">
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(burgers) }}
                {{ category(hotSandwiches) }}
            </div>
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(salads) }}
                {{ category(soup) }}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="fold-legend">Page 3 &mdash; kids, sides, drinks &amp; where to find us</p>

    {#- PAGE 3. The lightest of the three, deliberately: it is the page that
        ends up face-up on a table, so it carries the contact block rather
        than another dense column. Do not "fill" it by pulling a category
        forward from page 2 - the space IS the large print. -#}
    <div class="canvas lp-canvas">
        <header class="lp-head lp-head--run">
            <p class="p-display lp-runhead ink-teal">{{ site.name }} &middot; Menu</p>
            <p class="p-body lp-head-line lp-head-phone ink-teal">{{ site.phone }} &middot; {{ site.social.website }}</p>
        </header>

        <div class="lp-cols">
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(kids) }}
                {{ category(sides) }}
            </div>
            <div class="lp-col">
                {{ category(drinks) }}

                <div class="panel-spacer"></div>

                <div class="lp-visit">
                    <p class="p-label lp-foot-label ink-accent">Visit us</p>
                    <p class="p-display lp-visit-hours ink-teal">{{ site.opening.hoursDays }}</p>
                    <p class="p-display lp-visit-hours ink-teal">{{ site.opening.opens.time }} <span class="ampm">{{ site.opening.opens.meridiem }}</span> &ndash; {{ site.opening.closes.time }} <span class="ampm">{{ site.opening.closes.meridiem }}</span></p>
                    <p class="p-display lp-visit-phone ink-teal">{{ site.phone }}</p>
                    <p class="p-body lp-visit-line">{{ site.address.street }}</p>
                    <p class="p-body lp-visit-line">{{ site.address.locality }}, {{ site.address.region }} {{ site.address.postalCode }}</p>
                    <p class="p-body lp-visit-line">{{ site.social.website }}</p>
                </div>

                <div class="lp-foot">
                    <p class="p-label lp-foot-label ink-accent">Catering &amp; meal prep</p>
                    <p class="p-body lp-foot-body">Trays, box lunches and full-service catering for offices, churches, funerals and family gatherings. Call ahead or ask at the counter.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>
